# Decided
- Purposes, old -> new:
  - accessors.h: "obtain a node's relevant sub-expression, or the node
    itself, whichever grammar family the node belongs to." -> "reach a
    node's driving expression or what it carries, the node itself where
    nothing else drives it." trueqbit: "driving expression" (from the CTE
    work) encodes the caller's usage; the default to the node is the
    purpose, not a branch to cut. Option A of three.
  - field_predicates.h: "define the predicates on a column's field type
    that the type decides by itself." -> "define the field predicates read
    off the C++ type itself." trueqbit: the field axis asks whether a C++
    type qualifies for an SQL-level role (field_predicates_fwd.h); pass 1
    had revived "a column's field type" from the old @file line
    ("validity of fields of column nodes", 03f24a8b), a narrowing.
  - ddl_predicates.h: "... - a column, a table or an index." -> "decide
    what holds of the nodes of a schema definition." trueqbit: a column
    definition belongs to the schema definition, so
    is_pkcol_implicitly_insertable stays; the verb widens past
    "admissible elements".
  - recordset_predicates.h: "decide whether a node refers to a table or a
    record set, and so may occupy the record set slot of a factory." ->
    "decide which nodes stand for a record set."; the relation goes to the
    Intention, checked against the tree (ast/, builtin/, storage.h,
    exported).
  - field_predicates_concepts.h: "... whose condition is a concept, beside
    their C++17 fallback." -> "define the field predicates written as
    concepts."; the fallback goes to the Intention as the language's limit
    (trueqbit: they ought to be concepts; C++ concepts arrived with C++20).
  - node_fwd.h: "declare the concrete nodes that vocabulary files use
    directly without their definitions - in base-class overloads, partial
    specializations or metafunctions." -> "declare the nodes that are
    named before their definitions are in scope." The old reason was false
    for 5 of 9 (named only outside vocabulary/); trueqbit dropped
    "concrete".
  - node_traits.h / node_algorithms.h: "into one include" cut as shape;
    node_algorithms.h "the judgments that follow from what a node is" ->
    "what follows from what a node or a field type is and carries".
  - grammar_traits_fwd.h: leaf versus grouping production cut as variant;
    "the DSL nodes" -> "the types a DSL expression is built from", so the
    alias and UDF traits are covered.
  - nested_types.h: "through alias templates as syntactic sugar" cut.
  - vocabulary-layer.md: -> "lay down what belongs to the DSL vocabulary
    and where, and how it is specialized and read."
- trueqbit's rulings, not to be reopened:
  - A header's placement follows what it is, not who consumes it.
  - A vocabulary/ header groups a family of symbols by its file name, as
    in ast/, also where the family has one member today.
  - Parts and bases of nodes belong in node_fwd.h with the nodes; its
    namespace blocks keep the groups apart; splitting would scatter them
    for no gain. "The nodes" in its Purpose covers them; no widening.
  - field_predicates_concepts.h stays in the vocabulary: it answers a
    relation of schema nodes generically, so a consumer depends on nothing
    until it is in scope.
- Rejected: moving the hidden-column concepts to schema/ (Claude's lean);
  renaming them hidden_column_predicates.h (the reviewer's); renaming the
  field axis or splitting field_predicates*.h - "field" names where the
  axis began, and is_text_value belongs to it.
- Held as they were: argument_placeholders.h, clause_predicates.h,
  column_field_types.h, expression_element_predicates.h,
  field_predicates_fwd.h, index_filters.h, operand_predicates.h,
